{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 46, :comments ["<b>And the name of the daughter of Asher was Serach.</b> It should have said 'and the daughter of Asher was Serach.' Rather, this is what it says: Serach — she had the name of being a daughter of Asher, because she grew up in his house and was called by his name, but she was not his daughter rather his wife's daughter. And the Targum proves it: ושום בת אתת אשר סרח. And if you ask: who bore her? If one of the tribes bore her, why did he not mention his name upon her? And if a man of the world bore her, why was she traced back to Jacob our father? One may answer: because her name was especially well known for good deeds and piety, Scripture writes about her <b>and the name</b> — meaning, the name of the well-known and important woman. When they went down to Egypt, he counted Serach first, because she was older than her brothers. Here, where they were counted for the division of the land, he counted her last, because she took no portion in it. And Dinah, who had already died, he did not count."], :book :numbers, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:19:50.630701132Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}